  "method_card": {
    "steps": [
      "Identify the rounding position (d.p. or s.f.)",
      "Look at the next digit to the right",
      "If 5 or more, round up the target digit by 1; if less than 5, keep it",
      "Remove all digits after the rounding position (fill with zeros if before the decimal point)"
    ],
    "title": "How to Work with Decimals and Rounding",
    "content": "<p><strong>Decimals</strong> use place value columns after the decimal point: tenths, hundredths, thousandths. Understanding place value is essential for ordering, adding, subtracting, multiplying and dividing decimals.</p><p><strong>Rounding</strong> simplifies a number to a given degree of accuracy. Look at the <em>next digit</em> after the one you are rounding to: if it is 5 or more, round up; if it is less than 5, round down.</p><p><strong>Decimal places (d.p.):</strong> Count digits after the decimal point. <strong>Significant figures (s.f.):</strong> Count from the first non-zero digit. Leading zeros are never significant; trailing zeros after the decimal point are significant.</p><p><strong>Truncation</strong> means cutting off digits without rounding — simply remove everything after the specified place.</p><p><strong>Estimation</strong>: Round each number to 1 s.f. to get an approximate answer to a complex calculation.</p>",
    "example": "<p><strong>Round 0.04673 to 2 significant figures</strong></p><p><strong>Step 1:</strong> The first significant figure is 4 (ignore leading zeros). The second significant figure is 6.</p><p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Look at the next digit: 7 (≥ 5, so round up)</p><p><strong>Step 3:</strong> 6 rounds up to 7</p><p><strong>Answer:</strong> \\(0.047\\)</p>"
  },
